0243: Quantize the EFB for its copy only, and don't store colour that is overwritten unseen

The 6-bit blend resolve (0176) and the shadow-map caster pass both touch a full target worth of
pixels that nothing ever reads. Three amounts of work are dropped, pixel for pixel:

1. The quantize pass's LoadOp was Load (lib/gfx/efb_blend6.cpp, `runPass`). Its fragment shader is
   a full-screen triangle whose output covers every pixel and every sample of the attachment, so
   the load read a full target (7.06 MB at the user's 1680x1050) into tile memory to overwrite all
   of it. It is Clear now, in all three uses: the single-sampled quantize, the multisampled
   quantize, and the multisampled store.

2. A pass whose own colour copy is the last reader of its colour: the copy is a GXCopyTex over
   the whole target with clear, so the pass right after it clears the colour completely (0212
   already drops the resolve for exactly this case when the copy is a depth copy). The pixels then
   only have to reach the copy, so the resolve quantizes the colour straight into its scratch and
   the copy reads it from there (`efb_blend6::copy_source_over`, used by lib/gfx/encoding.cpp).
   That removes a full-target texture copy (read+write of the target) and the quantized copy back
   (load+read+store), which nothing would see: per resolve at 1680x1050, 35.3 MB before
   (copy 14.1 + load 7.06 + sample 7.06 + store 7.06), 14.1 MB after. Pixels outside the copy
   rectangle keep their unquantized values instead of being rewritten quantized; the next pass
   clears them without reading.

3. The same knowledge lets the shadow-map caster pass skip its colour store
   (lib/gfx/recording.cpp `resolve_pass_into`): its copy is a Z16 depth copy, the pass after it
   clears the colour completely, and no snapshot or scene post-op reads it, so its colour target is
   stored (7.06 MB) and never read. `StoreOp::Discard`. Gated off for multisampled targets (their
   resolve view is stored as well) and for offscreen passes.

At 4.4 resolves per frame the profile measured 6.222 ms for "EFB blend6 quantize"; the load
removal is 20% of a resolve's traffic and the scratch handoff 60% for the resolves that a clearing
copy ends (three of the four per frame in an ordinary room: the Z16 shadow copy is already dropped
by 0212). Expected 2.5-4 ms per frame. `AURORA_EFB_BLEND6_NODEFER=1` and the timing aid
`AURORA_EFB_BLEND6_TIMING=1` stay valid A/B switches; the "EFB blend6 copy" zone disappears for the
handoff resolves.

